# Auto-use System Gate and Sentinel

When the task touches architecture or security ship readiness, call these MCPs yourself. Do not wait for the user to name them.

## System Gate — architecture readiness

Call when any of these is true:
- Starting or changing system design, service boundaries, data stores, queues, auth topology, deploy topology
- User says ready to build, designed, architecture done, or asks whether the architecture holds
- Adding a new app/service/package that needs a declared flow
- Before claiming the design is complete

Workflow: `architecture_readiness` → if missing/incomplete, `architecture_start` then `architecture_record` from the real repo → `architecture_readiness` again. Fix record (or real mismatches) until PASS or a human decision is required.

Artefact: `.system-gate/architecture.json`. PASS ≠ scales. Never invent evidence or load reports.
CLI/CI: if `system-gate.config.json` has `"mode": "blocking"`, FAIL exits 1.

## Sentinel — security ship brake

Call when any of these is true:
- User says ship, merge, release, deploy, PR ready, or asks if it is safe to ship
- Adding auth, secrets, public APIs, Firebase/Supabase rules, Docker/K8s, security-impacting dependency upgrades
- After security-sensitive code changes in this session, before declaring done

Workflow: needed scans → `ship_readiness`. Prefer constellation-router `route_hint` then the named gate tools. CLI: `sentinel check --path .` for one-shot re-scan.

PASS ≠ secure forever. Never invent PASS; ERROR if scanners did not run.

## Routing law

- ready_to_build ↔ System Gate PASS
- ready_to_ship ↔ Sentinel PASS
- Neither implies the other.
- constellation-router never returns build/ship PASS — it only points at the gate to call.
